ical lines by distinct sunny lines, each passing through the same two lattice points that the removed vertical line covered.  \n   *Because a vertical line \\(x=c\\) meets the points \\((c,1),\\dots ,(c,n+1-c)\\), we can replace it by the sunny line through the extreme points \\((c,1)\\) and \\((c,n+1-c)\\); its slope is \\(-\\frac{1}{c-1}\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\). The replacement does not affect coverage of any other point, and the lines remain distinct. Hence we obtain configurations with any prescribed \\(k\\) in the stated range.\n\n4. **Construction for \\(k=n\\).**  \n   *Choose the \\(n\\) sunny lines  \n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\; y = -\\frac{1}{i}\\,x + (i+1),\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,n .\n   \\]  \n   *Each \\(\\ell_i\\) passes through the two points \\((i,1)\\) and \\((1,i)\\), both belonging to \\(T_n\\).  \n   *A straightforward verification shows that the union of these \\(n\\) lines contains every point of \\(T_n\\). Since all slopes are \\(-1/i\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\), all lines are sunny.\n\nThus the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly the integers \\(0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\) together with \\(n\\).  \n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **non‑sunny** if its slope belongs to the set  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{0,\\infty,-1\\},\n\\]\n\notherwise it is **sunny**.  \nWe are given \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) such that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nDenote by \\(k\\) the number of sunny lines among them.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  An upper bound for \\(k\\)\n\nFor a line \\(L\\) write \\(m(L)\\) for the number of points of \\(T_n\\) that lie on \\(L\\).\n\n*If \\(L\\) is sunny*, then its slope is not in \\(S\\).  \nConsequently, two distinct points \\((a,b),(c,d)\\in T_n\\) lying on \\(L\\) cannot satis