ot satisfy \\(a+b=c+d\\); otherwise the slope would be \\(-1\\). Hence a sunny line can contain at most one point from each \u201cdiagonal\u2019\u2019 set  \n\n\\[\nD_s=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=s\\},\\qquad s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1 .\n\\]\n\nSince there are exactly \\(n\\) such diagonals, we have  \n\n\\[\nm(L)\\le n\\qquad\\text{for every sunny line }L. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(L\\) is non\u2011sunny* then either it is horizontal, vertical, or has slope \\(-1\\).  \nIn each of these three cases the line can contain an entire diagonal \\(D_s\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\nm(L)\\ge 1\\quad\\text{and possibly }m(L)=|D_s|=s-1\\text{ for some }s. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nNow count incidences between the \\(n\\) lines and the \\(|T_n|\\) points:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n} m(L_i)=|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nLet the \\(k\\) sunny lines be \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) and the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines be non\u2011sunny.  \nUsing (1) and the trivial bound \\(m(L)\\ge 1\\) for non\u2011sunny lines we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{k} m(S_i)+\\sum_{j=1}^{n-k} m(N_j)\n\\le k\\cdot n+(n-k)\\cdot (n-1) .\n\\]\n\nBecause each non\u2011sunny line contains at most the largest diagonal, i.e. at most \\(n-1\\) points, we actually have the sharper inequality\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{k} m(S_i)+\\sum_{j=1}^{n-k} m(N_j)\n\\le kn+(n-k)(n-1). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nCombining (3) and (4) yields\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le kn+(n-k)(n-1)=n^2-nk .\n\\]\n\nRearranging gives\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2\\quad\\text{or}\\quad k=n .\n\\]\n\nThe case \\(k=n-1\\) would give \\(n^2-n(n-1)=n\\) on the right\u2011hand side, which is strictly smaller than \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\); hence \\(k=n-1\\) is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Constructions for \\(k=0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\)\n\nConsider the following family of \\(n\\) lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&V_c : x=c \\qquad (c=1,2,\\dots ,n-2),\\\\\n&D_2 : x+y=2,\\\\\n&D_{n+1} : x+y=n+1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe vertical line \\(V_c\\) contains exactly the points  \n\n\\[\n(c,1),\\;(c,2),\\dots ,\\bigl(c,n+1-c\\bigr),\n\\]\n\nall of which belong to \\(T_n\\).  \nThe diagonal \\(D_2\\) contains the single point \\((1,1)\\); the diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\) contains the points \\((1,n),\\,(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\).  \nA direct inspection shows that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines in (5).  \nThus (5) gives a configuration with \\(k=0\\).\n\nTo obtain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (\\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)) we replace any \\(k\\) of the vertical lines \\(V_c\\) by the sunny line that joins its two extreme points:\n\n\\[\nL_c :\\; \\text{the line through }(c,1)\\text{ and }(c,n+1-c).\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_c\\) is \\(-\\dfrac{1}{c-1}\\), which is never \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) for \\(c\\ge 2\\); hence \\(L_c\\) is sunny.  \nSince \\(L_c\\) still contains the same two extreme points as \\(V_c\\) and all intermediate points of \\(V_c\\) are already covered by the diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\), the covering property is preserved.  \nAll lines remain distinct because distinct values of \\(c\\) give distinct slopes.  \nConsequently we obtain, for each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\), a family of \\(n\\) lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Construction for \\(k=n\\)\n\nDefine, for \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,n\\),\n\n\\[\n\\ell_i :\\; y = -\\frac{1}{i}\\,x + (i+1).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(-\\frac{1}{i}\\) is never \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); therefore each \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.  \nEach \\(\\ell_i\\) passes through the two points \\((i,1)\\) and \\((1,i)\\), both belonging to \\(T_n\\).  \n\nWe claim that the union \\(\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}\\ell_i\\) contains every point of \\(T_n\\).  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\); then \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nIf \\(a=b\\) the point lies on the line \\(x=y\\), which is \\(\\ell_1\\).  \nIf \\(a\\neq b\\) assume without loss of generality \\(a<b\\).  \nSet \\(i=a\\); then \\((a,1)\\in\\ell_a\\) and the line \\(\\ell_a\\) has equation \\(y = -\\frac{1}{a}x + (a+1)\\). Substituting \\(x=a\\) gives \\(y=1\\); substituting \\(x=1\\) gives \\(y=a+1\\). Because the slope is negative, the line passes through all integer points \\((a,1), (a-1,2),\\dots ,(1,a+1)\\). Since \\(b\\le a+1\\) (otherwise \\(a+b>n+1\\)), the point \\((a,b)\\) appears among these, hence belongs to \\(\\ell_a\\).  \n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the sunny lines \\(\\ell_i\\), giving a configuration with \\(k=n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Conclusion\n\nWe have proved:\n\n* No configuration of \\(n\\) lines covering \\(T_n\\) can have exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n* For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (Section\u202f2).\n* There also exists a configuration with all \\(n\\) lines sunny (Section\u202f3).\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\cup\\{n\\}} .\n\\]"
