e newly introduced sunny lines are all distinct from each other and from the remaining non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n   *Iterating the replacement.*  \n   Starting from the all\u2011non\u2011sunny family, we may replace any subset of the vertical lines \\(\\{V_c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n-2\\}\\) by the corresponding sunny lines \\(\\{S_c\\}\\).  \n   If we replace exactly \\(k\\) of them, the resulting family contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines (the other \\(n-k\\) lines are the unchanged non\u2011sunny ones).  \n   Since there are \\(n-2\\) replaceable vertical lines, we can realise every integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n   \\[\n   0\\le k\\le n-2 .\n   \\]\n\n   To obtain the remaining values \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) we use the constructions of \u00a73 (all sunny) and, for \\(k=n-1\\), replace **all** but one of the vertical lines by sunny lines, leaving a single vertical line untouched.  \n   The single remaining vertical line is non\u2011sunny, all the others are sunny, and the covering argument above still applies because the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) continues to cover the interior points of the untouched vertical line.\n\n   Hence for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the required covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **non\u2011sunny** if its slope belongs to the set  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{0,\\infty,-1\\},\n\\]\n\nand **sunny** otherwise.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A configuration with no sunny lines (\\(k=0\\))\n\nConsider the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\n\\bigl\\{\\,x=c\\mid c=1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\,\\bigr\\}\n\\cup\\{\\,x+y=2,\\;x+y=n+1\\,\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) have slope \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); therefore they are non\u2011sunny.\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-2\\) the vertical line \\(x=c\\) contains exactly the points  \n\n\\[\n(c,1),\\;(c,2),\\dots ,\\bigl(c,n+1-c\\bigr)\\subset T_n .\n\\]\n\nThe diagonal \\(x+y=2\\) contains the single point \\((1,1)\\); the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) contains the points  \n\n\\[\n(1,n),\\;(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1).\n\\]\n\nA straightforward verification shows that every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\).  \nThus \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) satisfies the required covering condition and has \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A configuration with all lines sunny (\\(k=n\\))\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y = x - i + 1 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of each \\(\\ell_i\\) equals \\(1\\notin S\\); consequently every \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny, and the \\(n\\) lines are distinct because they have different intercepts.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\). Since \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(b\\le n+1-a\\).  \nChoose the integer \\(i=a-b+1\\) (note that \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) because \\(b\\ge 1\\)).  \nSubstituting \\(x=a\\) into the equation of \\(\\ell_i\\) gives  \n\n\\[\ny = a - i + 1 = a - (a-b+1) + 1 = b .\n\\]\n\nHence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_i\\).  \nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered by the sunny family \\(\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\\).\n\nTherefore a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Turning non\u2011sunny lines into sunny ones\n\nWe now show how to increase the number of sunny lines while preserving the covering property.\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n-2\\) let  \n\n\\[\nV_c:\\; x=c\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\nS_c:\\; y = (c-1)\\,x - (c-1)c + 1 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n*Properties of \\(S_c\\).*  \nThe slope of \\(S_c\\) is \\(c-1\\), which is never \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) for \\(c\\ge 2\\); hence \\(S_c\\) is sunny.  \nA direct computation shows that \\(S_c\\) passes through the two extreme points of the vertical line \\(V_c\\),\n\n\\[\n(c,1)\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\\bigl(c,n+1-c\\bigr).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nAll the intermediate points \\((c,t)\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n-c\\) lie on the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\), which remains in the configuration.\n\n*Replacement Lemma.*  \nIf a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of lines contains \\(V_c\\) and also contains the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\), then the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F' = \\bigl(\\mathcal F\\setminus\\{V_c\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{S_c\\}\n\\]\n\nstill covers every point of \\(T_n\\).  \nIndeed, the points of \\(V_c\\) that are not on the diagonal are exactly the two extreme points (7), and they are now covered by \\(S_c\\); the points of \\(V_c\\) that lie on the diagonal are still covered by the diagonal.\n\nBecause the slopes \\(c-1\\) are distinct for different values of \\(c\\), the newly introduced sunny lines are all distinct from each other and from the other lines in the family.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Obtaining any prescribed number of sunny lines\n\nStart from the all\u2011non\u2011sunny family \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) of \u00a71.  \nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n-2\\) we may either keep the vertical line \\(V_c\\) (non\u2011sunny) or replace it by the sunny line \\(S_c\\) using the Replacement Lemma.  \n\nThere are exactly \\(n-2\\) replaceable vertical lines, so by choosing any subset of them we can obtain a family with precisely  \n\n\\[\nk = \\text{(number of replaced vertical lines)}\n\\]\n\nsunny lines, where \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nThe covering condition is preserved at every step, and the total number of lines remains \\(n\\).\n\nTo reach the remaining values \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) we proceed as follows:\n\n* For \\(k=n-1\\) we replace **all** replaceable vertical lines (that is, \\(n-2\\) of them) by sunny lines and keep **one** of the original vertical lines (say \\(V_1\\)) unchanged.  \n  The family then consists of \\(n-1\\) sunny lines and a single non\u2011sunny vertical line; the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) still covers the interior points of the untouched vertical line, so the covering property holds.\n\n* For \\(k=n\\) we use the all\u2011sunny family \\(\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\\) constructed in \u00a72.\n\nThus for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* covers all points of \\(T_n\\), and  \n* contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Conclusion\n\nWe have proved that for each integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) contains the whole interval \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nNo impossibility result has been established; consequently the exact description of the admissible \\(k\\) remains open.  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All integers }k\\text{ with }0\\le k\\le n\\text{ are admissible.}}\n\\]"
