cup\\;\\{\\,y=1,\\;x+y=n+1\\,\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) have slope \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); hence they are non‑sunny.\nWe verify that \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) covers \\(T_n\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\le n-2\\)*, the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal L_0\\).\n\n*If \\(a=n-1\\)*, then \\(b\\le 2\\) because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n – For \\(b=1\\) the point is on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).  \n – For \\(b=2\\) we have \\(a+b=n+1\\), so the point lies on the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\n*If \\(a=n\\)*, the inequality forces \\(b=1\\); this point is again on \\(y=1\\).\n\nThus every element of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\), and \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct non‑sunny lines. Hence a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A configuration with all lines sunny (\\(k=n\\))\n\nFor each \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) let \\(\\ell_a\\) be the line through the two points  \n\n\\[\nP_a=(a,1),\\qquad Q_a=(1,n+2-a).\n\\]\n\nThe equation of \\(\\ell_a\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\ell_a:\\; y-1=\\frac{n+1-a}{1-a}\\,(x-a)\n\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad\ny=\\frac{-(n+1-a)}{a-1}\\,x+\\frac{n+1}{a-1}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{n+1-a}{1-a}= -\\frac{n+1-a}{a-1}\\) is never \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) for any admissible \\(a\\); therefore each \\(\\ell_a\\) is sunny.  \nThe \\(n\\) lines are distinct because they have different \\(x\\)-intercepts.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\). Since \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(b\\le n+1-a\\).  \nAll points of the vertical column \\(\\{(a,t)\\mid 1\\le t\\le n+1-a\\}\\) lie on \\(\\ell_a\\) (by construction \\(\\ell_a\\) contains \\((a,1)\\) and \\((a,n+1-a)\\), hence the whole segment). Consequently \\((a,b)\\) belongs to \\(\\ell_a\\).  \n\nThus the family \\(\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) sunny lines and covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Replacement Lemma\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n-2\\) define  \n\n\\[\nV_c:\\;x=c,\\qquad \nS_c:\\; y=(c-1)x-(c-1)c+1 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n*Properties of \\(S_c\\).*  \nThe slope of \\(S_c\\) equals \\(c-1\\), which is not in \\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\); hence \\(S_c\\) is sunny.  \nA direct substitution shows that \\(S_c\\) passes through the two extreme points of the vertical line \\(V_c\\):\n\n\\[\nS_c\\ni (c,1),\\qquad S_c\\ni\\bigl(c,n+1-c\\bigr).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nAll intermediate points \\((c,t)\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n-c\\) satisfy \\(c+t=n+1\\) and therefore lie on the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\n*Lemma.*  \nLet \\(\\mathcal F\\) be a family of lines that contains \\(V_c\\) and also contains the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \nThen the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F'=(\\mathcal F\\setminus\\{V_c\\})\\cup\\{S_c\\}\n\\]\n\nstill covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe points of \\(V_c\\) not on the diagonal are exactly the two extreme points (6); they are now covered by \\(S_c\\).  \nPoints of \\(V_c\\) that lie on the diagonal remain covered because the diagonal is still present.  \nAll other points of \\(T_n\\) are untouched, so coverage is preserved. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Obtaining any number \\(k\\) of sunny lines\n\nStart from the \\(k=0\\) family \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) of (3).  \nFor each \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) we may either keep the vertical line \\(V_c\\) (non‑sunny) or replace it by the sunny line \\(S_c\\) using the Replacement Lemma.  \n\nThere are exactly \\(n-2\\) replaceable vertical lines, so by choosing any subset of them we obtain a family with precisely  \n\n\\[\nk=\\text{(number of replacements)}\n\\]\n\nsunny lines, where \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nThe total number of lines remains \\(n\\) because each replacement swaps one line for another.\n\n*Re