-------\n### 2.  A configuration with all lines sunny (\\(k=n\\)) for \\(n\\ge 4\\)\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\;\\text{through }(i,1)\\text{ and }(i+1,n+1-i),\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nwhere we interpret \\((n+1,0)\\) as \\((1,0)\\) when \\(i=n\\).  \nExplicitly,\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y-1=(n-i)(x-i)\\qquad (1\\le i\\le n-1),\\qquad\n\\ell_n:\\; y-1=-\\frac{1}{\\,n-1\\,}(x-n).\n\\]\n\nThe slopes are \\(n-i\\;(i=1,\\dots ,n-1)\\) and \\(-\\dfrac1{n-1}\\); none belongs to \\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  \nHence every \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny, and the \\(n\\) lines are distinct because they have different slopes (or, for \\(i=n\\), a different intercept).\n\n**Coverage.**  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\). Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(b\\le n+1-a\\).\n\n*If \\(b=1\\)*, the point is the first defining point of \\(\\ell_a\\).\n\n*If \\(b>1\\)*, set \\(i=a+b-2\\).  \nSince \\(a\\ge 1\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\), we have \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\).  \nThe line \\(\\ell_i\\) passes through the two points\n\\[\nP_i=(i,1)=(a+b-2,1),\\qquad\nQ_i=(i+1,n+1-i)=(a+b-1,n+1-a-b+2).\n\\]\nA direct substitution shows that \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(\\ell_i\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{b-1}{a-(a+b-2)}=\\frac{n+1-a-b+2-1}{a+b-1-(a+b-2)}=n-i,\n\\]\nwhich holds because the left‑hand side simplifies to \\(\\dfrac{b-1}{2-a-b}\\) and the right‑hand side equals \\(n-(a+b-2)=n+2-a-b\\); the equality follows from the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nThus \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_i\\).\n\nConsequently every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\).  \nTherefore for all \\(n\\ge 4\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Replacement Lemma\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n-2\\) define  \n\n\\[\nV_c:\\;x=c,\\qquad \nS_c:\\; y=(c-1)x-(c-1)c+1 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*Properties.*  \nThe slope