ble number of sunny lines is therefore \\(2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Final answer\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{If }n\\ge 4:\\qquad k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\};\\\\\n&\\text{If }n=3:\\qquad\\; k\\in\\{0,1,2\\}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]\n\nThe constructions above prove that every listed value of \\(k\\) can indeed be realised, and the impossibility of \\(k=n\\) when \\(n=3\\) follows from a simple case‑by‑case verification (any sunny line misses at least one of the three extreme points \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\), so three sunny lines cannot cover all six points). This completes the solution."
